<title>AUSTRALIA: Australian stocks seen softer as Wall St weakens.</title>
<headline>Australian stocks seen softer as Wall St weakens.</headline>
<text>
<p>-----------------(Snapshot 8.35 a.m./2235 GMT)-----------------</p>
<p>AORD cl	   2445.80   +3.40   DJIA  cl	6660.21  -43.34</p>
<p>SPI Sycom cl	 2422     -15   US 30yr cl	 7.09   +0.04</p>
<p>10yrbond futs    92.100  -0.015   Gold NY 342.95 Lon fix 342.20</p>
<p>AUD	   0.7775 (Mon 0.7765)   CRB Index cl   247.61   +0.27</p>
<p>NIKKEI cl	18551.66 +199.52   FTSE cl	 4328.70  +18.20</p>
<p>S&amp;P500(Globex)   764.35   +0.20   NZSE40	  2253.13   +0.83</p>
<p>--------------------------(April 22)---------------------------</p>
<p>*Stocks seen softer after Wall St weakens, volumes to stay thin. &quot;I expect we will be down as well but nothing drastic. Volumes will still be thin,&quot; said Reynolds and Co's Peter Struk.  </p>
<p>*Pre-open bids A$17.10 (-.02), A$16.76</p>
<p>(-.034), A$6.04 (-.01), A$7.82 (steady)</p>
<p>*Wall Street slumped lower as higher interest rates and a sell-off in tech stocks drained momentum from the market.</p>
<p>*ADRs: News Corp 18-5/8 (-1/8), BHP 26-3/8</p>
<p>(-3/8), WMC 24-3/8 (+1/4), National Australia Bank 65 (+1-1/8)</p>
<p>*Gold price was higher. On LME, copper finished virtually unchanged, most others followed copper's lead in holding to narrow price bands and showing little direction.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
